{"records":[{"id":"pj624p7dk8","title":"MYO1F Positions cGAS on the Plasma Membrane to Ensure Full and Functional Signaling","description":"Cyclic GMP–AMP synthase (cGAS) recognizes viral or endogenous DNA to activate the innate immune response to infection and autoimmune diseases. After binding double-stranded DNA, cGAS synthesizes 2′3′ cGMP–AMP, which triggers the production of type I interferons and proinflammatory cytokines. In addition to its cellular localization in the cytosol and nucleus, cGAS is also present in the plasma membrane, but the functional significance and regulatory mechanism of its membrane localization are still unclear. Here, we report that some cGAS localizes to the plasma membrane by binding to MYO1F. Upon viral infection, SYK-mediated MYO1F phosphorylation at specific sites promotes the recruitment of acetyltransferase KAT2A, which further acetylates cGAS at lysine residues 421, 292, and 131 on the plasma membrane, and this acetylation is essential for its full activation. In addition, we showed that the membrane localization of cGAS is critical for virus‒cell fusion-triggered signalling activation and type I interferon production due to Mn2+ release from membrane-enclosed organelles into the cytosol. Our results show that MYO1F-mediated cGAS membrane localization is critical for its full activation in response to viral infection and virus‒cell fusion.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-12-11T16:44:25.555Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pj624p7dk8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pj624p7dk8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"heping","last_name":"wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chenhui","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microscopy","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"g7g3gh6rdm","title":"Enhancement of the transduction efficiency of a lentiviral vector for neuronspecific retrograde gene delivery through the point mutation of fusion glycoprotein type E.","description":"Variants of fusion glycoprotein type E enhancing transduction efficiency of a lentiviral vector for neuron-specific retrograde gene delivery.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-10-14T18:12:49.66Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/g7g3gh6rdm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/g7g3gh6rdm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Shigeki","last_name":"Kato","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kazuto","last_name":"Kobayashi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Scientific Databases"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"c3w9br288b","title":"The dystonia-associated Torsin1A sustains CLCC1 function in membrane fusion of the nuclear envelope for NPC biogenesis. Maslennikova et al.","description":"DYT1 dystonia is an incurable movement disorder caused by a loss-of-function mutation in Torsin1A, an\nendoplasmic reticulum (ER)-resident AAA+-ATPase. Here, we use Drosophila and human cells to shed\nlight on Torsins’ mode of action. Fly germ cells lacking dTorsin arrest in development with defects in\nnuclear pore complex (NPC) biogenesis due to impaired nuclear envelope membrane fusion. We identify\nthe conserved membrane protein Chloride Channel CLIC-like protein 1 (CLCC1) as a Torsin1A interaction\npartner whose absence phenocopies membrane fusion defects caused by Torsin deletion. CLCC1 is\nenriched at membrane fusion sites, and molecular dynamics (MD) simulations suggest that CLCC1 rings\ninduce bilayer remodeling and lipid flux to initiate fusion of the outer and inner nuclear membranes.\nRemarkably, CLCC1 overexpression rescues defects associated with loss of Torsins, indicating that a main\nrole of dTorsin/Torsin1A is to sustain CLCC1 functionality. Our findings inform a model of nuclear\nenvelope membrane fusion and imply that modulating CLCC1 expression is a promising therapeutic\nprospect for DYT1 dystonia.\n\nPlease, use the free available software Fiji/ImageJ to open the files.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-30T09:55:28.922Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/c3w9br288b","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/c3w9br288b"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daria","last_name":"Maslennikova","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"52gxfr4h7d","title":"Endocytosis is essential for cysteine deprivation-induced ferroptosis","description":"WB raw data","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-15T17:31:18.573Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/52gxfr4h7d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/52gxfr4h7d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"xuesong","last_name":"liu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Death"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"x83zcrfz7t","title":"A lipid flippase shapes the ciliary membrane and is essential for ciliogenesis","description":"A lipid flippase shapes the ciliary membrane and is essential for ciliogenesis","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-29T13:19:45.294Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/x83zcrfz7t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/x83zcrfz7t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhengmao","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qingqing","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Junmin","last_name":"pan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5s2np3c4y7","title":"Endocytosis is essential for cysteine deprivation-induced ferroptosis","description":"WB raw data","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-15T18:44:30.385Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5s2np3c4y7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5s2np3c4y7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"xuesong","last_name":"liu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Death"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ph2xvpxs2y","title":"DNA damage promotes TMPRSS2-ERG oncoprotein destruction and prostate cancer suppression via signaling converged by GSK3β and WEE1. Hong et al","description":"TMPRSS2-ERG gene fusion occurs in approximately 50% of prostate cancer (PCa) and the fusion product is a key driver of prostate oncogenesis. However, how to leverage cellular signaling to ablate TMPRSS2-ERG oncoprotein for PCa treatment remains elusive. Here, we demonstrate that DNA damage induces proteasomal degradation of wild-type ERG and TMPRSS2-ERG oncoprotein through ERG threonine-187 and tyrosine-190 phosphorylation mediated by GSK3β and WEE1, respectively. The dual phosphorylation triggers ERG recognition and degradation by the E3 ubiquitin ligase FBW7 in a manner independent of a canonical degron. DNA damage-induced TMPRSS2-ERG degradation was abolished by cancer-associated PTEN deletion or GSK3β inactivation. Blockade of DNA damage-induced TMPRSS2-ERG oncoprotein degradation causes chemotherapy-resistant growth of fusion-positive PCa cells in culture and in mice. Our findings uncover a previously unrecognized TMPRSS2-ERG protein destruction mechanism and demonstrate that intact PTEN and GSK3β signaling are essential for effective targeting of ERG protein by genotoxic therapeutics in fusion-positive PCa.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-18T22:45:00.674Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ph2xvpxs2y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ph2xvpxs2y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Haojie","last_name":"Huang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7t9snvwv3p","title":"Early fusion intermediate of ACE2-using coronavirus spike acting as antiviral target ","description":"Coronavirus fusion with and entry into the host cell depends on viral spike, which acts as a crucial component of viral infection. However, the lack of receptor-activated spike intermediate conformation has hindered a comprehensive understanding of spike-induced membrane fusion. Here, we captured an ACE2-induced early fusion intermediate conformation (E-FIC) of SARS-CoV-2 spike in which HR1 in S2 has ejected while S1 remains attached. This E-FIC can transition to the late fusion intermediate conformation after S2' cleavage. Leveraging this discovery, we designed an E-FIC-targeted dual-functional antiviral protein AL5E. AL5E effectively inactivated ACE2-using coronaviruses and inhibited their infection, outperforming a mono-functional antiviral in protecting animals against these coronaviruses. This study has identified the E-FIC and used it as target for the development of a dual-functional antiviral for the prevention and treatment of ACE2-using coronavirus infection. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-22T17:50:04.485Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7t9snvwv3p","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7t9snvwv3p"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lixiao","last_name":"Xing","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhimin","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xinling","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qianying","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qiyu","last_name":"Mao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiang","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aihua","last_name":"Hao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shuai","last_name":"Xia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zezhong","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lujia","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Guangxu","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qian","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhenguo","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shibo","last_name":"Jiang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lei","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lu","last_name":"Lu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Membrane Fusion","Viral Entry","Antiviral Agent Active against Respiratory Viruses","Coronavirus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k7w5tg3b7y","title":"Data for: In-Silico Drug Discovery for Covid19 by Targeting Spike Glycoprotein of Sars Cov - 2 (Wuhan Corona Virus 2019 Outbreak) Against the Docking Analysis with Structure Predicted Human ‘Ace2-Fc Region of Igg1’ Fusion Protein as a Protein Based Drug.","description":"\n\nAbstract:The globe was frightened with a newly isolated virus named as sars cov 2 formally called as novel corona virus 2019.This work is predicting a protein based drug for sars cov 2.A virus was outbreaks at wuhan city of china on December 2019.The RNA sequence of that virsus was published in NCBI.(NCBI Ref Sequence:NC_045512.2)We collected the serface glycoprotein sequences from NCBI and genbank and predicted the protein structure with swiss model and phyre 2 protein structure prediction tools.The predicted spike glycoprotein was have a docking function with the human ACE2 receptor.We predicted the 3D structure of ACE2- FC region of IgG1 fusion protein and docked with virus spike glycoprotein and found a good docking potential of fusion protein with Sars Cov 2.ACE2- FC region of IgG1 will be use as a protein drug against\n SarsCoV 2.\n\n2020 Ijcrt / volume 8,issue 2, February 2020 / ISSN:2320-2882\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-03-04T20:13:07.255Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k7w5tg3b7y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k7w5tg3b7y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Pandiya Muthu kumaran","last_name":"Boopathirajan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Drug Discovery","Fusion Protein","In Silico Biology","Clinical Virology"],"journal_issn":["15565068"]},{"id":"7nx4c8s76r","title":"Recruitment of tetraspanin TSP-15 regulates plasma membrane repair in C. elegans epidermis","description":"Maintaining the integrity of the plasma membrane after cellular damage is essential for cell survival. However, it is unclear how cells repair large membrane injuries in vivo. In this study, we report that the tetraspanin protein, TSP-15, is recruited to large membrane wounds and forms a ring-like structure in C. elegans syncytial epidermis and promotes membrane repair after an injury. TSP-15 recruits from the adjacent region underneath the plasma membrane to the wound site in a RAB-5-dependent manner upon membrane damage. Genetic and live imaging analysis suggested that the endosomal sorting complex required for transport III (ESCRT III) is essential for recruiting TSP-15 from the early endosome to the damaged membrane. Moreover, TSP-15 interacts with and is required for the accumulation of the t-SNARE protein Syntaxin-2, which facilitates membrane repair. These findings suggest that recruiting TSP-15 to the wounded membrane provides novel insights into the cellular repair of large wounds resulting from environmental insults. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-04-27T20:20:19.854Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7nx4c8s76r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7nx4c8s76r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Suhong","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":135233,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":165,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":135233,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":134540,"restricted":68,"embargoed":625},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2247},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":699},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":464},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":422},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":411},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":397},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":396},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":335},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":263},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":228}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":132627},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1875},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":178},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":137},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":79},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":56},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":39},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":28}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd24